Drafting an edict of the Tang dynasty conferring upon Left Vice Director of the Department of State Affairs Fang Xuanling 房玄齡 the additional title of Junior Preceptor of the Heir Apparent [13th year of the Zhenguan reign (639)].
Drafting a memorial of thanks of the Song dynasty on appointing Cheng Yi 程頤 as Court Gentleman for Comprehensive Duty and Expositor-in-Waiting of the Chongzheng Palace [1st year of the Yuanyou reign (1086)].
Legal Judgments (判語) [five items]:
Colleagues signing and endorsing official documents on another's behalf;
Manufacturing utensils, cloth, and silk not in accordance with legal standards;
Incumbent officials arbitrarily erecting stone steles for themselves;
Concealing the natural increase of government livestock;
Ordinary persons stealing money and grain from government treasuries and granaries.

Third Session
Policy Questions (策) [five items]
Question: A former Confucian scholar said: "That by which Yao 堯 and Shun 舜 served as models for ten thousand generations was simply following this human nature." Since antiquity, when sage emperors and enlightened kings ruled over All Under Heaven, the means by which they cultivated their persons, regulated their families, governed their states, and brought peace to All Under Heaven assuredly could not depart from this nature. As for their words handed down to future generations to establish teachings, which are found recorded in the Classics and commentaries—can you cite and discuss them? Reverently considering our Dynasty, where successive sacred emperors have inherited the throne one after another, establishing the great principles and setting forth the regulations, they have purely employed this Way to transform and perfect All Under Heaven. …
